135:Observation 2 In a single pointer dereference we can travel at least halfway
136-from u to LA(u, d), for any d. Finding the appropriate pointer takes O(1) time.
137-
138-Proof. We let δ = depth(u) − d. We can travel up by δ, which is at least δ/2.
139-The pointer to follow is simply Jumpu [log δ].
140-   (Note that since the ﬂoor and log operations are word computations, the
141-algorithm is a RAM algorithm.)
142-
143:      As a consequence of Observation 2, we obtain the following lemma:
144-
145-Lemma 3. The Jump-Pointers Algorithm solves the Level Ancestor Problem in
146-time O(n log n), O(log n).
147-
148-Proof. To achieve O(n log n) preprocessing, we apply a trivial dynamic program.
149-To answer query LevelAncestorT (u, d) in O(log n) time, we repeatedly follow
150-the pointers that will get us halfway to LAT (u, d). Therefore after at most log n
151-jumps, we locate LAT (u, d).
152-                                      The Level Ancestor Problem Simplified      511
153-
154-3.3    The Ladder Algorithm: An O(n), O(log n) Solution
155-In the Ladder Algorithm, we decompose the tree T into (nondisjoint) paths,
156-which we call ladders because they help us climb up the tree. Our choice of how
157-we do the decomposition may seem peculiar at ﬁrst, but it is an integral part of
158-the fast algorithms.
159-   To understand why it is advantageous to break the tree into paths, observe
160-that solving the level ancestor problem on a single path of length n is trivial.
161-Observation 4 On a path of length n, the Level-Ancestor Problem can trivially
162-be solved with (optimal) complexity O(n), O(1).
163-Proof. We maintain a Ladder array Ladder[0 . . . n − 1], where the i-th
164-array position corresponds to the depth-i node on the path. To answer
165-LevelAncestorT (u, d), we return Ladder[d], which takes O(1) time.
166-    We now describe the ladder decomposition of the tree T , which proceeds in
167-two stages: The ﬁrst stage requires us to ﬁnd a long-path decomposition of the
168-tree T , which greedily decomposes the tree into disjoint paths.
169-
170-Stage 1: Long-Path Decomposition. We greedily break T into long disjoint paths
171-as follows. We ﬁnd a longest root-leaf path in T , breaking ties arbitrarily, and
172-remove it from the tree. This removal breaks the remaining tree into subtrees
173-T1 , T2 , . . .. We recursively split these subtrees by removing their longest paths.
